# Pages that are deliberately over the 8 KB ceiling, and why.
#
# The ceiling exists because every byte of a page is paid by the agent that opens
# it. A page is normally split when it exceeds the ceiling. These two are not,
# because splitting them would cost the reader more than the bytes do: each is
# opened to look one thing up, and the answer would land on the other side of the
# split as often as not.
#
# An exception is not free. A page listed here is reported on every run, so the
# pressure to shrink it stays visible. An entry naming a page that does not exist,
# or one that is already under the ceiling, is a defect and is reported.
#
#   <page> = <bytes> # reason
#
22-state-machine.md = 15360  # the whole .fsml format, and it cannot be half-learned: History, OnFinal, Submachine, the guard grammar and the transition rules answer each other. The only other place any of them is written down is a 50 KB schema that gives the grammar and not the meaning, and a run that had to go there carried 13,000 tokens of it for sixty turns
51-debug.md         = 12288  # a symptom index; a split makes the reader guess which half their symptom is in. "Running the pieces" already came out of it as 50-running.md. Raised once when the generator began printing rule numbers: the refusal section now shows the message shape it must be read against, which is what stops the reader guessing at a finding instead of quoting it
